---

**Q1 — Private from outside**

```java
class Counter {
    private int count = 0;
    public void increment() { count++; }
}

// In a different class:
Counter c = new Counter();
c.count = 5;
```
*What happens?*

A) Compiles and runs; `c.count` is now 5.
B) Compile error — `count` is private.
C) Runtime error — access to private field throws an exception.
D) Compiles with a warning; the assignment is silently ignored.

*Correct:* B. Baseline question — establishes that visibility is enforced *at compile time*, not at runtime. The (C) distractor is important: students with a runtime-checking mental model (perhaps from JavaScript or Python) need to update it. The (D) distractor catches anyone with a "warnings are fine" mindset that you may want to disrupt early.

The teaching beat is short: visibility modifiers are a compile-time discipline. The compiler refuses to generate bytecode that violates them. By the time your program is running, the question is settled.

---

**Q2 — Protected outside the package**

```java
// File: animals/Animal.java
package animals;
public class Animal {
    protected String name;
}

// File: pets/Dog.java
package pets;
import animals.Animal;

public class Dog extends Animal {
    void rename(Animal other) {
        other.name = "Rex";   // line A
        this.name = "Rex";    // line B
    }
}
```
*Which lines compile?*

A) Both A and B
B) B only
C) A only
D) Neither

*Correct:* B. This is the question that catches almost everyone, because students think `protected` means "subclasses can access it, period." It doesn't. The actual rule: a subclass can access a protected member *through a reference of its own type (or a subtype)*. It cannot access protected members on a reference of the parent type — even though `Dog` *is* an `Animal`.

Wrong models:
- (A) — "I'm in a subclass, so I can touch any `Animal`'s protected fields." This is the canonical wrong model and it's *almost* right. Worth naming explicitly: "protected access is granted to the subclass *as an implementation channel for its own instances*, not as a license to peer into arbitrary parent-type references."
- (C) — likely a guess, or "`this` is special and somehow more restricted."
- (D) — overcorrected "protected basically doesn't work cross-package."

The teaching beat: protected is the most subtle of the four modifiers. The intent is that a subclass can complete its parent's behavior, not that subclasses form a privileged peer group. This is also a good moment to mention that this subtlety is *one of the reasons your design principles prefer composition* — composition has no analogue of protected, so the question doesn't arise.

---

**Q3 — Package-private**

```java
// File: shapes/Circle.java
package shapes;
public class Circle {
    double radius;   // no modifier
    public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
}

// File: shapes/CircleFactory.java
package shapes;
public class CircleFactory {
    public Circle make(double r) {
        Circle c = new Circle();
        c.radius = r;     // line A
        return c;
    }
}

// File: app/Main.java
package app;
import shapes.Circle;
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Circle c = new Circle();
        c.radius = 5.0;   // line B
    }
}
```
*Which lines compile?*

A) Both A and B
B) A only
C) B only
D) Neither

*Correct:* B. No modifier means package-private — accessible within the same package, denied outside. `CircleFactory` is in the same package and sees `radius`; `Main` is not and doesn't.

Wrong models:
- (A) — thinks "no modifier" means public, or that the default is permissive. A common Python/JavaScript carryover.
- (C) — backwards: thinks the default is private. Also plausible if they're pattern-matching on "absence of `public` means restricted."
- (D) — thinks `radius` is somehow inaccessible from both contexts. Suggests they're missing the existence of a default visibility level entirely.

This is the cleanest question in the set — there's not much subtlety, just a fact to know. But it's worth asking because the answer determines whether students realize Java has *four* visibility levels, not three. Many of them will not have noticed.

---

**Q4 — Final boss: private is per-class, not per-instance**

```java
class Account {
    private double balance;

    public Account(double initial) {
        this.balance = initial;
    }

    public static double transfer(Account from, Account to, double amount) {
        from.balance -= amount;     // line A
        to.balance += amount;       // line B
        return from.balance;
    }
}
```
*What happens when this code compiles and runs?*

A) Compile error on line A — `static` methods cannot access non-static fields.
B) Compile error on lines A and B — `balance` is private; only methods called on the instance via `this` can access it.
C) Compile error on line B only — you can read `from.balance` but writing to `to.balance` requires a setter.
D) Compiles and runs correctly; both lines access `balance` through the references directly.

*Correct:* D. This is the question that disrupts the most deeply-held wrong model in the set: students believe `private` means "accessible only through `this`." It doesn't. `private` means *visible only within the lexical scope of the enclosing class*. Any code inside `Account`'s class body can access *any* `Account`'s private fields, regardless of whether the reference is `this`, a parameter, a local variable, or pulled out of a collection.

Wrong models, each worth naming:

- (A) — confuses two orthogonal axes. Static-vs-instance is about *how the method is dispatched*. Public-vs-private is about *who can see the name*. They don't interact. A static method is just a regular method that doesn't get a `this`; it has the same access rights as any other code in the class.

- (B) — the canonical wrong model. "Private means encapsulated per object." Worth a long teaching beat: encapsulation in Java is *per class*, not per object. The phrase to give them: "Java's `private` is class-private, not object-private." (Contrast with languages like Smalltalk, where private is per-instance — if students have seen that, name the difference.)

- (C) — invents a getter/setter rule that doesn't exist in the language. Suggests they've been taught "always use getters and setters" as a *language rule* rather than a *design convention*. Worth disrupting: the convention exists for design reasons (which your principles will reinforce), but the language imposes no such requirement.

- (D) — correct, and the teaching beat is the payoff.

The synthesis: this is *why* `equals` can be implemented efficiently. When you write `this.balance == other.balance` inside `Account.equals(Account other)`, you're not going through a getter — you're directly reading another instance's private field, because you're in the same class. This same principle is what makes copy constructors, factory methods, and comparators-defined-inside-the-class clean to write.

And then — this is the pedagogical money — *this is exactly the access pattern your factory-method-on-a-separate-utility-class design choice deliberately gives up*. When you put a factory in a different class, you no longer get free access to the backing class's privates; you have to go through the public interface. That's a *deliberate* discipline, paid for in exchange for clearer separation of concerns. The fact that Java would *let* you put the factory in the same class and access privates directly is what makes the choice meaningful.

---

A pacing note: Q4 is genuinely hard and probably warrants the full PI cycle — initial poll, peer discussion, re-poll, then reveal. The misconception in (B) is *load-bearing* in many students' mental models. If you don't dislodge it cleanly here, it'll keep generating wrong predictions for the rest of the semester.

You could also use Q4 as a transition into your design principles for the week. "The language permits this. Our design principles will often forbid it. Why?" sets up the rest of the unit as motivated rather than arbitrary.

